@*
    The data-exposure warning, in one place so the two screens that show it cannot drift apart.
    Compact renders the one-line version above a conversation; the full form sits on the settings page.
*@

<div class="note warn">
    @if (this.Compact)
    {
        <span>
            This assistant reads <strong>every table in every connection</strong> configured here, and what it
            reads is sent to @this.ProviderLabel. It is read-only. Nothing is sent until you send a message.
        </span>
        @if (this.OnDismiss.HasDelegate)
        {
            <button class="btn ghost sm" type="button" @onclick="this.OnDismiss">Got it</button>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        <strong>What the assistant can see</strong>
        <ul style="margin: 6px 0 0; padding-left: 18px">
            <li>
                It can read <strong>every table in every connection</strong> configured in this tool — not only
                this one. Cross-connection questions are the point ("is this order id in staging too?"), and the
                cost is that the reach is that wide.
            </li>
            <li>
                What it reads — schemas, field names, and document contents — is sent to
                @this.ProviderLabel as part of the conversation. Treat it the way you would treat pasting
                the same rows into that provider's own web UI.
            </li>
            <li>
                It is <strong>read-only</strong>. It has no tool that can insert, update or delete anything, so
                it cannot change your data even if asked to.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It never decrypts.</strong> Fields protected by DocumentDb's field-level encryption reach
                the model as the ciphertext they are stored as, even on a connection you have given read keys to.
                That is deliberate and not configurable: ciphertext arriving at a model provider is harmless,
                and a decrypted SSN arriving there is a breach.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Nothing is sent until you send a message.</strong> There is no background indexing, no
                schema pre-fetch and no telemetry — configuring the assistant does not, by itself, transmit
                anything. Every request happens because you typed one.
            </li>
            <li>
                Connection strings and passwords are never included. The assistant can see that a connection
                exists and what backend it is, not how to reach it.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin: 8px 0 0">
            If none of that is acceptable for a given database, leave the assistant switched off for it — or
            point it at a local model with the <strong>OpenAI-compatible</strong> provider, which keeps the
            data on this machine.
        </p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The one-line form, for above a conversation.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Compact { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The configured provider, so the warning names where the data actually goes.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? ProviderName { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Supplied by the chat banner; omit and no dismiss button renders.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnDismiss { get; set; }

    string ProviderLabel => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(this.ProviderName)
        ? "the AI provider you configure"
        : this.ProviderName;
}
